      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We should probably be careful in the cleanup path(of a duplicate vcpu_id failure) to not unconidtionally zero out the pid_table in vmx_vcpu_free\n\nGood point... So it seems like instead of zeroing pid_table when freeing vCPU (aosp/4151696), we should forbid freeing vCPUs earlier than when destroying the VM? (Thus, again, keep it more aligned with the host KVM.)\n\n\u003e Also not sure if the delayed setting of pid_table after attach would cause any race. Is the vcpu usable after an attach that could cause the vcpu to run(by a malicious host) before finishing postcreate?\n\nGood point... In the host case, I see the existing `kvm_arch_vcpu_postcreate()` already takes `vcpu-\u003emutex`, so it seems we can prevent races with vcpu run etc if we add our vcpu_postcreate call there, under `vcpu-\u003emutex` locked?\n\nIn the pKVM case, I see two options (neither seems quite elegant):\n1. Call vcpu_postcreate inside `attach_pkvm_vcpu_to_vm()`, _before_ setting the vcpu refcount to 1.\n2. Call vcpu_postcreate after `attach_pkvm_vcpu_to_vm()` but call `pkvm_vcpu_load()` before it and `pkvm_vcpu_put()` after it (thus prevent loading this vCPU on another CPU in parallel and thus prevent vcpu_run and other hypercalls from doing anything with this vCPU on another CPU in parallel). This is more tricky, since `pkvm_vcpu_load()` and `pkvm_vcpu_put()` may fail.",
      "parentUuid": "ad5030a4_62767d04",
      "revId": "3bf049dd7d9d09e0677a18256c8f1d551204ff35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
